nodecode-obol is a rewrite of UEddy's UEddy/Obol:

  https://github.com/UEddy/Obol
  commit 87015c996b608b93e81f8123011c5bf3d2d53f71
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 UEddy (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin was a Hermes plugin for invoicing from a chat. It
made invoice calls against Stripe's REST API with the user's own key over httpx,
pinned the API version, looked a customer up by email, created the invoice and its
items, finalized and sent it, kept a local SQLite cache and a hash-chained activity
ledger, and ran deterministic risk checks (duplicate, unusual amount, lookalike
domain, changed payout details) in its approval hook. This cell keeps that shape in
Common Lisp: the same seven invoice operations as tools, the same Stripe endpoints
and form encoding, the same customer-by-email reuse, the same draft-then-send split,
the same refusal of a live key unless live mode is allowed, the same duplicate and
unusual-amount checks, and a ledger of what was billed in a JSON file under the
Nodecode home. The approval prompt that Hermes showed on every write is expressed
here in the tools' own descriptions and in the draft default, because Nodecode has
no per-call approval seam to hook.

What was left out and why: the draft_from_message tool, which called Hermes's
configured model through ctx.llm (a cell has no such call, so the model extracts the
fields itself); the pre_tool_call approval hook (no Nodecode counterpart); the
dashboard tab and its polling backend (the ledger reads through the command and the
list tool); SQLite and the hash-chained logs (one JSON ledger is enough for the same
answers); the lookalike-domain and Stripe radar/dispute checks, so two of the four
risk checks survive; and the bank-transfer PDF invoices, which needed a PDF writer.
